### **[PATTERN: API-ROUTES]** Organizing API Routes by Domain

**Structure API routes by business domain, not HTTP method:**

```
src/app/api/
├── clients/                     # Client management
│   ├── route.ts                # GET /api/clients, POST /api/clients
│   ├── [id]/
│   │   ├── route.ts            # GET, PATCH, DELETE /api/clients/:id
│   │   └── documents/
│   │       └── route.ts        # GET, POST /api/clients/:id/documents
│   └── import/
│       └── route.ts            # POST /api/clients/import
│
├── service-relationships/       # Service relationship management
│   ├── route.ts                # GET, POST /api/service-relationships
│   ├── [id]/
│   │   ├── route.ts            # GET, PATCH /api/service-relationships/:id
│   │   ├── messages/
│   │   │   └── route.ts        # GET, POST messages for service
│   │   └── issues/
│   │       └── route.ts        # GET, POST issues for service
│
├── referrals/                   # Referral management
│   ├── route.ts
│   ├── [id]/
│   │   └── route.ts
│   └── drafts/
│       └── route.ts
│
├── organizations/               # Organization management
│   ├── route.ts
│   └── [id]/
│       ├── route.ts
│       ├── members/
│       │   └── route.ts
│       └── settings/
│           └── route.ts
│
└── auth/                        # Authentication
    ├── signin/
    ├── signup/
    └── callback/
```

**API Route Naming Rules:**
- Use **plural nouns** for collections: `/api/clients`, `/api/providers`
- Use **nested routes** for relationships: `/api/clients/[id]/documents`
- Use **action verbs** for non-CRUD operations: `/api/clients/import`, `/api/service-relationships/[id]/accept`
- Keep routes **shallow** (max 3-4 levels deep)

