## 🔒 Security Requirements

### **[CRITICAL: SECURITY-FIRST]** Security is NON-NEGOTIABLE

**Every feature MUST include:**

1. **Authentication**
   ```typescript
   const session = await getServerSession(authOptions);
   if (!session?.user) {
     // Reject request
   }
   ```

2. **Authorization** (organization + role)
   ```typescript
   const { db } = await connectToDatabase();
   const profile = await db.collection('user_profiles').findOne({
     userId: new ObjectId(session.user.id),
   });
   
   // Check role if needed
   if (!['case_manager', 'supervisor'].includes(profile?.role)) {
     // Reject request
   }
   ```

3. **Data Isolation**
   - EVERY query MUST filter by organizationId
   - Use MongoDB query filters for organization isolation
   - Test data isolation thoroughly

4. **Input Validation**
   - Use Zod schemas for all inputs
   - Sanitize user-provided content
   - Validate file uploads (type, size)
   - Never trust client-side validation alone

5. **Data Access**
   - Never expose sensitive data in client components
   - Redact PII in logs
   - Use environment variables for secrets
   - Never commit .env files

**Security Checklist for Every Feature:**
- [ ] Authentication required (NextAuth session)
- [ ] User's organizationId verified
- [ ] Role-based permissions checked (if applicable)
- [ ] MongoDB queries filter by organizationId (prevent cross-org data access)
- [ ] All inputs validated with Zod
- [ ] No NoSQL injection vulnerabilities
- [ ] No XSS vulnerabilities
- [ ] Sensitive data encrypted at rest
- [ ] HTTPS enforced in production
- [ ] Rate limiting considered

---

## 🎯 **[PHILOSOPHY: NO-OVER-ENGINEERING]** Production-Grade Without Fragmentation

### Core Principle: **Simplicity First, Extract When Necessary**

**Production-grade does NOT mean:**
- ❌ Creating a component for every 20 lines of JSX
- ❌ Extracting every function into a separate file
- ❌ Fragmenting readable code into incomprehensible pieces
- ❌ Premature abstraction "just in case"
- ❌ Turning 500-line components into 50 tiny files

**Production-grade DOES mean:**
- ✅ Clean, readable code with clear responsibilities
- ✅ Extracting when logic is **complex, reusable, or testable**
- ✅ Keeping related code together when it aids comprehension
- ✅ Strategic separation that improves maintainability
- ✅ Balance between organization and practical simplicity
- ✅ **Highly secure, efficient, optimized, and performance-focused**
- ✅ **Cost-conscious architecture (don't make things unnecessarily expensive)**

---

### **[RULE: EXTRACT-WISELY]** When to Extract vs Keep Inline

#### ✅ **EXTRACT to a Component When:**

1. **Reusability**: Used in 3+ places
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Reused across multiple tables
   export const StatusBadge = ({ status }) => { /* ... */ };
   ```

2. **Complexity**: 100+ lines of complex logic
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: AuthorizationCell had 111 lines of complex tooltip logic
   export const AuthorizationCell = ({ authorization }) => { /* ... */ };
   ```

3. **Testability**: Needs isolated unit tests
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Complex validation needs testing
   export const PaymentForm = ({ onSubmit }) => { /* ... */ };
   ```

4. **Domain Isolation**: Clear single responsibility
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Search has its own state and logic
   export const SearchBar = ({ onSearch }) => { /* ... */ };
   ```

#### ❌ **KEEP INLINE When:**

1. **Single Use**: Only used once in parent
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Keep inline, clear in context
   return (
     <div>
       {items.map(item => (
         <div key={item.id} className="...">
           {/* 30 lines of item-specific JSX - KEEP HERE */}
         </div>
       ))}
     </div>
   );
   ```

2. **Simple Mapping**: Straightforward data display
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Don't extract simple row rendering
   {services.map(svc => (
     <TableRow key={svc.id}>
       <TableCell>{svc.name}</TableCell>
       <TableCell>{svc.status}</TableCell>
     </TableRow>
   ))}
   ```

3. **Tightly Coupled**: Needs parent's state/context
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Keep inline - uses parent's handlers directly
   <button onClick={() => handleItemAction(item.id, 'approve')}>
     {item.status === 'pending' ? 'Approve' : 'Re-approve'}
   </button>
   ```

4. **Visual Clarity**: Breaking it up reduces readability
   ```typescript
   // ❌ Bad: Over-fragmentation
   <ClientRow client={client} />
   <ServiceRow service={service} />
   <ActionRow actions={actions} />
   
   // ✅ Good: Keep together for visual flow
   <div className="client-card">
     <div className="client-header">{client.name}</div>
     <div className="client-services">{service.name}</div>
     <div className="client-actions">{actions}</div>
   </div>
   ```

---

### **Extract Utilities/Hooks When:**

#### ✅ **YES - Extract to Utils/Hooks:**

1. **Business Logic**: Calculations, transformations
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Complex calculation extracted
   // utils/calculations.ts
   export function calculateAuthSummary(services) {
     // 50 lines of complex logic
   }
   ```

2. **Reusable State**: Used in multiple components
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Shared state management
   // hooks/use-table-state.ts
   export function useTableState() {
     const [filters, setFilters] = useState(/*...*/);
     const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState(/*...*/);
     return { filters, setFilters, sortBy, setSortBy };
   }
   ```

3. **API Calls**: Data fetching logic
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Centralized data fetching
   // hooks/use-clients.ts
   export function useClients() {
     return useSWR('/api/clients', fetcher);
   }
   ```

#### ❌ **NO - Keep Inline:**

1. **Simple Functions**: 5-line helpers used once
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Keep inline
   const fullName = `${client.firstName} ${client.lastName}`;
   
   // ❌ Bad: Don't extract
   // utils/names.ts
   export const getFullName = (first, last) => `${first} ${last}`;
   ```

2. **Single-Use Handlers**: Component-specific logic
   ```typescript
   // ✅ Good: Keep in component
   const handleSubmit = async (e) => {
     e.preventDefault();
     await saveData(formData);
     toast.success('Saved');
   };
   ```

---

### **Real-World Example: ClientTableView Refactoring**

**❌ Over-Engineered Approach (AVOID):**
```
ClientTableView/
├── ClientTableView.tsx (50 lines - TOO fragmented)
├── ClientSummaryRow.tsx
├── ClientNameCell.tsx
├── ClientProviderCell.tsx
├── ClientStatusCell.tsx
├── ServiceRow.tsx
├── ServiceNameCell.tsx
├── ServiceStatusCell.tsx
├── ServiceAuthCell.tsx
├── ServiceActionCell.tsx
└── ... 20 more tiny files
```
**Problem**: Component becomes a puzzle. Following logic requires opening 20 files.

**✅ Production-Grade Approach (CORRECT):**
```
ClientTableView/
├── ClientTableView.tsx (1,045 lines - clean, readable)
│   ↳ Inline: Client rows, service rows (clear in context)
├── components/
│   ├── AuthorizationCell.tsx (complex 111-line logic)
│   ├── ServiceActionsMenu.tsx (reusable dropdown)
│   └── ClientTableModals.tsx (modal consolidation)
├── hooks/
│   ├── use-client-table-data.ts (data fetching)
│   ├── use-client-table-state.ts (UI state)
│   └── use-status-updater.ts (API calls)
└── utils/
    ├── calculations.ts (business logic)
    └── colors.ts (constants)
```
**Why Better**: Row rendering stays inline (readable), only complex pieces extracted.

---

### **Decision Tree: Should I Extract?**

```
Is the code complex (100+ lines)?
├─ YES → Extract to component
└─ NO → Is it reused 3+ places?
    ├─ YES → Extract to component
    └─ NO → Is it pure business logic?
        ├─ YES → Extract to util function
        └─ NO → Keep inline (it's clear where it is)
```

---

### **Anti-Patterns to Avoid**

#### ❌ **Micro-Components** (Every 20 Lines)
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Over-fragmented
const ClientName = ({ name }) => <span>{name}</span>;
const ClientEmail = ({ email }) => <span>{email}</span>;
const ClientPhone = ({ phone }) => <span>{phone}</span>;

// ✅ GOOD: Keep together
<div className="client-info">
  <span>{client.name}</span>
  <span>{client.email}</span>
  <span>{client.phone}</span>
</div>
```

#### ❌ **Premature Abstraction**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: "Future-proofing" that never gets used
const useGenericTableStateManager = <T extends BaseEntity>() => {
  // 200 lines of unused abstraction
};

// ✅ GOOD: Solve current problem simply
const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ search: '' });
```

#### ❌ **Utility Explosion**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Utils for everything
// utils/string-utils.ts
export const capitalize = (s) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
export const lowercase = (s) => s.toLowerCase();
export const trim = (s) => s.trim();

// ✅ GOOD: Use native methods
const name = client.name.trim().toLowerCase();
```

---

### **Guiding Questions Before Extracting**

1. **Readability**: Does extraction make code easier or harder to follow?
2. **Reusability**: Will this be used in 3+ places realistically?
3. **Testability**: Does this need isolated unit tests?
4. **Coupling**: Is this tightly coupled to parent state?
5. **Size**: Is this 100+ lines of complex logic?

**If 3+ answers are "YES" → Extract. Otherwise → Keep inline.**

---

### **Key Takeaway**

> **"Production-grade" means clean, maintainable, secure code.
> It does NOT mean fragmenting everything into micro-pieces.
> Extract strategically, keep code readable, optimize for comprehension."**

**ALWAYS ask: "Does this extraction make the codebase better, or just more complex?"**

---

## ⚡ **Performance & Efficiency Without Over-Engineering**

### Core Principle: **Fast, Secure, Cost-Effective**

**High-quality code should be:**
- 🔒 **Secure** - Authentication, authorization, input validation (non-negotiable)
- ⚡ **Performant** - Fast load times, optimized queries, efficient rendering
- 💰 **Cost-Conscious** - Don't use expensive solutions when simple ones work
- 🧹 **Clean** - Readable, maintainable, well-structured
- ✅ **Tested** - Works correctly, handles edge cases

---

### **✅ DO: Smart Performance Optimizations**

#### 1. **Database Queries: Efficient by Default**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Single query with aggregation
const clients = await db.collection('clients').aggregate([
  { $match: { organizationId } },
  { $lookup: { from: 'services', localField: '_id', foreignField: 'clientId', as: 'services' } },
  { $limit: 100 }
]).toArray();

// ❌ BAD: N+1 queries (expensive, slow)
const clients = await db.collection('clients').find({ organizationId }).toArray();
for (const client of clients) {
  client.services = await db.collection('services').find({ clientId: client._id }).toArray();
}
```

#### 2. **React: Memoize Expensive Calculations**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Memoize heavy calculations
const sortedClients = useMemo(() => 
  clients.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
  [clients]
);

// ✅ GOOD: Memoize callbacks passed to children
const handleUpdate = useCallback((id: string) => {
  updateClient(id);
}, [updateClient]);

// ❌ BAD: Recalculate on every render
const sortedClients = clients.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
```

#### 3. **API Routes: Proper Caching & Pagination**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Paginated with caching headers
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  const { searchParams } = new URL(request.url);
  const page = parseInt(searchParams.get('page') || '1');
  const limit = 50;
  
  const data = await fetchData({ page, limit });
  
  return NextResponse.json(data, {
    headers: {
      'Cache-Control': 'public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=120',
    },
  });
}

// ❌ BAD: Load all data, no caching
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  const allData = await db.collection('items').find({}).toArray(); // Could be 10,000+ items!
  return NextResponse.json(allData);
}
```

#### 4. **Images: Optimize, Don't Over-Serve**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Next.js Image with optimization
<Image
  src="/avatar.jpg"
  alt="Avatar"
  width={48}
  height={48}
  quality={85}
  placeholder="blur"
/>

// ❌ BAD: Full resolution image for thumbnail
<img src="/5mb-photo.jpg" className="w-12 h-12" />
```

---

### **❌ DON'T: Over-Optimize or Over-Spend**

#### 1. **Don't Use Expensive Services for Simple Tasks**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Using Redis for session storage (adds cost + complexity)
// When MongoDB sessions work fine for < 10k users
import Redis from 'ioredis';
const redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL);

// ✅ GOOD: Use existing MongoDB for sessions
// Simple, cost-effective, already in your stack
const session = await db.collection('sessions').findOne({ userId });
```

#### 2. **Don't Premature Optimize**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Complex caching for infrequently accessed data
const cache = new Map();
async function getUser(id: string) {
  if (cache.has(id)) return cache.get(id);
  const user = await db.collection('users').findOne({ _id: id });
  cache.set(id, user);
  setTimeout(() => cache.delete(id), 60000);
  return user;
}

// ✅ GOOD: Simple query first, optimize later if needed
async function getUser(id: string) {
  return await db.collection('users').findOne({ _id: id });
  // MongoDB is already fast with indexes
}
```

#### 3. **Don't Over-Engineer State Management**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Redux for simple local state (adds complexity, bundle size)
const store = configureStore({
  reducer: { filters: filtersReducer, sorting: sortingReducer }
});

// ✅ GOOD: Simple useState for local state
const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ search: '' });
const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState('name');
```

#### 4. **Don't Use Heavy Libraries for Simple Tasks**
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Import entire lodash (70kb) for one function
import _ from 'lodash';
const unique = _.uniq(array);

// ✅ GOOD: Native JS or import specific function
const unique = [...new Set(array)];
// OR
import uniq from 'lodash/uniq'; // Import only what you need
```

---

### **Performance Checklist**

**Before Adding Any Optimization:**
- [ ] Is this actually a bottleneck? (Measure first)
- [ ] Will this noticeably improve user experience?
- [ ] Is the complexity worth the gain?
- [ ] Does it add cost (API calls, cloud services, etc.)?
- [ ] Can I solve this with simpler code?

**Database Queries:**
- [ ] Using indexes on frequently queried fields
- [ ] Limiting results with `.limit()`
- [ ] Using aggregation instead of multiple queries
- [ ] Selecting only needed fields (`.select()` or `$project`)

**React Components:**
- [ ] Using `useMemo` for expensive calculations
- [ ] Using `useCallback` for callbacks passed to children
- [ ] Using `React.memo` for expensive pure components
- [ ] Not over-memoizing (adds memory overhead)

**API Routes:**
- [ ] Proper HTTP caching headers
- [ ] Pagination for large datasets
- [ ] Compression enabled (Next.js does this by default)
- [ ] Rate limiting for public endpoints

**Bundle Size:**
- [ ] Tree-shaking unused code
- [ ] Dynamic imports for heavy components
- [ ] Not importing entire libraries for one function
- [ ] Using Next.js built-in optimizations

---

### **Real-World Performance Examples**

#### **Before: Slow & Expensive**
```typescript
// ❌ 500ms+ load time, 50 API calls
function ClientDashboard() {
  const [clients, setClients] = useState([]);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    async function load() {
      const clients = await fetch('/api/clients').then(r => r.json());
      for (const client of clients) {
        client.services = await fetch(`/api/clients/${client.id}/services`).then(r => r.json());
        client.issues = await fetch(`/api/clients/${client.id}/issues`).then(r => r.json());
      }
      setClients(clients);
    }
    load();
  }, []);
  
  return <Table data={clients} />;
}
```

#### **After: Fast & Efficient**
```typescript
// ✅ 50ms load time, 1 API call
function ClientDashboard() {
  const { data: clients, isLoading } = useSWR(
    '/api/clients?include=services,issues',
    fetcher,
    { revalidateOnFocus: false }
  );
  
  const sortedClients = useMemo(() => 
    clients?.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
    [clients]
  );
  
  if (isLoading) return <Skeleton />;
  return <Table data={sortedClients} />;
}
```

**What Changed:**
- Single API call with joined data (server-side)
- SWR for caching + automatic revalidation
- Memoized sorting calculation
- Proper loading state

---

### **Cost-Consciousness Principles**

**Choose Simple Over Complex:**
- ✅ MongoDB for data + sessions (already have it)
- ❌ MongoDB + Redis + ElasticSearch (3x the cost)

**Choose Efficient Over Expensive:**
- ✅ Next.js Image optimization (built-in, free)
- ❌ External image CDN ($50/month for 10k images)

**Choose Proven Over Shiny:**
- ✅ SWR for data fetching (battle-tested, lightweight)
- ❌ New experimental library (unknown reliability)

**Scale When You Need It:**
- ✅ Start with simple MongoDB queries
- ✅ Add indexes when queries slow down
- ✅ Add caching when load increases
- ❌ Don't architect for 1M users when you have 10

---

### **Key Takeaway**

> **"Performance means fast, secure, and cost-effective.
> Measure before optimizing. Use simple solutions first.
> Don't add complexity or cost without clear benefits."**

**ALWAYS ask: "Does this optimization improve the user experience enough to justify the complexity/cost?"**

---

## 💻 Code Quality Standards

### TypeScript

**Strict Mode - NO EXCEPTIONS:**
```typescript
// tsconfig.json has strict: true

// ❌ NEVER use 'any'
function process(data: any) { }

// ✅ Use proper types or 'unknown'
function process(data: ClientData) { }
function process(data: unknown) { 
  if (isClientData(data)) {
    // Type guard
  }
}

// ✅ Define interfaces for all data structures
interface Client {
  id: string;
  first_name: string;
  last_name: string;
  email: string | null;
  organization_id: string;
  created_at: string;
  updated_at: string;
}

// ✅ Use discriminated unions for variants
type ActionResult<T> = 
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string };

// ✅ Use const assertions for literals
const REFERRAL_STATUSES = ['pending', 'accepted', 'rejected'] as const;
type ReferralStatus = typeof REFERRAL_STATUSES[number];
```

### Naming Conventions

```typescript
// Files
client-card.tsx              // Components (kebab-case)
client-actions.ts            // Actions/utilities (kebab-case)
use-client-data.ts           // Hooks (kebab-case, starts with 'use-')

// Components
export function ClientCard() { }        // PascalCase
export function AddClientModal() { }    // PascalCase

// Functions
function calculateTotal() { }           // camelCase
async function fetchClientData() { }    // camelCase

// Constants
const MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024;  // UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
const API_BASE_URL = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL;

// Types/Interfaces
interface UserProfile { }               // PascalCase
type ReferralStatus = string;           // PascalCase

// Database
clients                                 // snake_case (tables)
organization_id                         // snake_case (columns)
created_at                             // snake_case (columns)
```

### Code Organization

```
src/
├── app/                          # Next.js App Router
│   ├── (auth)/                  # Route group (auth required)
│   │   ├── case-manager/        # Case manager pages
│   │   ├── provider/            # Provider pages
│   │   └── supervisor/          # Supervisor pages
│   ├── api/                     # API routes
│   │   └── [resource]/          # Resource-based routes
│   ├── login/                   # Public pages
│   └── layout.tsx               # Root layout
│
├── components/                   # React components
│   ├── ui/                      # shadcn/ui base components
│   ├── dashboard/               # Dashboard components
│   ├── clients/                 # Client management
│   ├── referrals/              # Referral management
│   ├── providers/              # Provider directory
│   └── layout/                 # Layout components
│
├── lib/                         # Utilities and business logic
│   ├── supabase/               # Supabase clients
│   │   ├── client.ts           # Client-side
│   │   ├── server.ts           # Server-side
│   │   └── middleware.ts       # Middleware
│   ├── actions/                # Server actions
│   ├── utils/                  # Helper functions
│   └── hooks/                  # Custom React hooks
│
└── types/                       # TypeScript definitions
    ├── database.ts             # Database types
    └── index.ts                # Shared types
```

### Error Handling

**Always handle errors gracefully:**
```typescript
// API Routes
try {
  // Operation
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Detailed error for debugging:', error);
  
  // Return user-friendly error
  return NextResponse.json(
    { error: 'Something went wrong. Please try again.' },
    { status: 500 }
  );
}

// Client Components
try {
  // Operation
  toast.success('Operation completed');
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error);
  toast.error('Operation failed. Please try again.');
}

// Server Actions
try {
  // Operation
  return { success: true, data };
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error);
  return { 
    success: false, 
    error: 'Operation failed. Please try again.' 
  };
}
```

**Error Handling Principles:**
- Log detailed errors server-side
- Show user-friendly messages client-side
- Never expose stack traces to users
- Never expose database errors to users
- Provide actionable error messages
- Use proper HTTP status codes
- Handle network failures gracefully

---

## 🎨 UI/UX Standards

### Design System

**Use shadcn/ui components consistently:**
```typescript
// ✅ Use shadcn components
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/components/ui/card';
import { Dialog, DialogContent } from '@/components/ui/dialog';
import { Input } from '@/components/ui/input';
import { Label } from '@/components/ui/label';

// ❌ Don't create custom buttons/inputs unless absolutely necessary
<button className="...">  // No, use <Button>
<input className="..." />  // No, use <Input>
```

### Component Styling Standards (CRITICAL)

**ALWAYS refer to `UI_COMPONENT_STANDARDS.md` for proper styling of:**
- Select dropdowns (must have `bg-white border-2` on Trigger and Content)
- AlertDialog/Modal dialogs (must have `z-[9999] bg-white border-2 shadow-2xl` on Content)
- Inputs/Textareas (must have `bg-white border-2`)

**Key Rules:**
1. **All AlertDialog/Dialog components** MUST use `z-[9999]` on the Content to appear above overlays
2. **All Select dropdowns** MUST have `bg-white` on both Trigger and Content
3. **All SelectItem components** MUST have `cursor-pointer` class
4. **Never use z-index between 100-9999** (use 50 for dropdowns, 9999 for modals)

**Example (correct):**
```typescript
// AlertDialog - ALWAYS use these classes
<AlertDialog open={isOpen} onOpenChange={setIsOpen}>
  <AlertDialogContent className="z-[9999] bg-white border-2 shadow-2xl">
    {/* ... */}
  </AlertDialogContent>
</AlertDialog>

// Select - ALWAYS use these classes
<Select>
  <SelectTrigger className="h-9 text-sm bg-white border-2 hover:border-slate-300">
    <SelectValue />
  </SelectTrigger>
  <SelectContent className="bg-white border-2 shadow-lg z-50">
    <SelectItem value="x" className="cursor-pointer">X</SelectItem>
  </SelectContent>
</Select>
```

### Responsive Design

**Mobile-first approach:**
```typescript
// ✅ Mobile-first responsive classes
<div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
  {/* Content */}
</div>

<div className="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-4">
  {/* Content */}
</div>

// ✅ Hide on mobile, show on desktop
<div className="hidden md:block">Desktop only</div>

// ✅ Show on mobile, hide on desktop
<div className="block md:hidden">Mobile only</div>
```

**Responsive breakpoints:**
- `sm`: 640px (tablets)
- `md`: 768px (tablets/small laptops)
- `lg`: 1024px (laptops)
- `xl`: 1280px (desktops)
- `2xl`: 1536px (large desktops)

### Loading States

**ALWAYS implement loading states:**
```typescript
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
import { Loader2 } from 'lucide-react';

export function ActionButton() {
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  async function handleClick() {
    setLoading(true);
    try {
      await performAction();
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  }

  return (
    <Button onClick={handleClick} disabled={loading}>
      {loading && <Loader2 className="mr-2 h-4 w-4 animate-spin" />}
      {loading ? 'Processing...' : 'Submit'}
    </Button>
  );
}
```

### Empty States

**ALWAYS implement empty states:**
```typescript
export function ClientList({ clients }: { clients: Client[] }) {
  if (clients.length === 0) {
    return (
      <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center py-12 text-center">
        <Users className="h-12 w-12 text-muted-foreground mb-4" />
        <h3 className="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">No clients yet</h3>
        <p className="text-muted-foreground mb-4">
          Get started by adding your first client.
        </p>
        <Button>Add Client</Button>
      </div>
    );
  }

  return (
    <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {clients.map(client => <ClientCard key={client.id} client={client} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Accessibility

**WCAG 2.1 AA compliance:**
- Use semantic HTML (`<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, etc.)
- Add ARIA labels to interactive elements
- Ensure sufficient color contrast (4.5:1 for text)
- Support keyboard navigation (Tab, Enter, Esc)
- Add focus indicators
- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3)
- Provide alt text for images
- Ensure form labels are properly associated

```typescript
// ✅ Good accessibility
<Button aria-label="Delete client">
  <Trash2 className="h-4 w-4" />
</Button>

<Dialog>
  <DialogContent aria-describedby="dialog-description">
    <DialogTitle>Confirm Action</DialogTitle>
    <p id="dialog-description">Are you sure you want to proceed?</p>
  </DialogContent>
</Dialog>
```

